{
  "requirement": "Repository validation MUST continue after a failed check and report every failed\ncheck it finds in one run, each as a {term}`requirements.ci.validation_error`.\nThis is a convention for every validation error; the requirement for each check\nstates which conditions that check reports. A file that cannot be parsed, such as\na malformed `habeas.toml`, `book.toml`, or evidence file, is one failed check:\nits error MAY report only the first parse or decoding problem in that file, and\nchecks that do not depend on its contents MUST still run.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: false.** The orchestrator, its tests and the behaviour for malformed files are sound. The gaps are in how completely the implementation is marked, and in one CLI path that appears to abort early.\n\n**What works**\n- `inspect_repository_with_progress` (src/evidence.rs:172-301) is marked `{impl}` for this requirement. It collects errors into one `Vec<String>` and never returns early on a failed check.\n  - A `habeas.toml` load failure is pushed as an error, then validation continues.\n  - `book_source_dir` falls back to `md` after a bad `book.toml`, so definition collection still runs.\n  - Each evidence file is validated independently. Orphan and missing-evidence checks, reference scanning, reference validation and Rust coverage all still run.\n- A malformed evidence file is one failed check. `validate_evidence_file` returns after the first JSON error (lines 582-715), which the \"MAY report only the first parse problem\" clause allows. Other files and the global checks still run.\n- Tests:\n  - `configuration_and_dependency_failures_do_not_stop_later_checks` (src/evidence.rs:2065) covers four things. A bad `habeas.toml` and a bad `book.toml` are each reported alongside a bad evidence JSON file and a reference error. Every unresolvable term is reported. Other records' errors still appear.\n  - `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` (src/evidence.rs:1774) checks many distinct errors in one run.\n  - `inspection_progress_preserves_validation_and_finishes_each_phase` (src/evidence.rs:1570) checks that a broken evidence file and a broken Rust source file don't stop later phases.\n  - Together these cover the interesting cases.\n\n**Gaps**\n1. **Incomplete `{impl}` marking.** Only the orchestrator and `DefinitionKind::noun` carry `{impl}` for `requirements.ci.errors`. Other code implements the obligation but doesn't cite it:\n   - `close_dependencies` (src/terms.rs:124-153) is the code the repo's own audit says was changed for this requirement (`audits/requirement-rewordings/ci-references-definitions.md`, \"Code fixes made instead of rewording\"). It \"continu[es] past each problem so every one is found\", yet it is marked only for `requirements.definitions.dependencies`.\n   - `validate_dependencies` (src/terms.rs:155) is marked only for `requirements.definitions.selection` and `requirements.definitions.dependencies`.\n   - The following also implement the continue-after-failure behaviour without citing this requirement:\n     - `book_source_dir`\n     - the `continue` paths in `collect_definitions`\n     - `files_with_extension`\n     - `validate_evidence_file`\n   - Under the marking rule, partial implementation without an `{impl}` reference is a gap.\n2. **Possible CLI early abort.** `src/main.rs` `main` calls `load_workspace_evidence(&current_dir)?` before every command except the `mdbook supports`, `setup` and `hook` commands. This includes `status`, which renders the validation report. A malformed `habeas.toml` would therefore end `habeas status` with an error before the other checks run. The \"checks that do not depend on its contents MUST still run\" clause is exercised only through `validate_repository`, not through this CLI path. I did not check whether `status` is meant to be exempt.\n\nThere are no FIXME markers acknowledging these gaps, so the result is `false` rather than FIXME.",
  "references": "45047f68cc536eea05d5b16aca566fe249e75f3544f0bf1fd6934f8a0eb8ecdb",
